artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a social organization for which empirical data is collected about its constituent agents and their activities (for example, communication logs, transaction records, co-membership records, or sentiment-bearing exchanges). A modeling framework for social resilience requires as input one or more of the following types of networks on the agent set:

(i) interaction networks, whose edges encode pairwise interactions weighted by intensity or frequency;
(ii) temporal networks, in which edges carry time stamps or activation intervals;
(iii) multilayer networks, in which several edge types (layers) coexist on the same vertex set;
(iv) signed networks, whose edges carry positive or negative signs reflecting cooperative or antagonistic relations.

Raw data of the kinds above cannot serve directly as model input: it must be preprocessed (filtered, aggregated, thresholded, disambiguated, projected from bipartite or hyperedge form, time-binned, sign-attributed, etc.) before a network is obtained. Different preprocessing choices lead to substantially different networks and hence to different downstream statistical conclusions and resilience estimates.

Problem. Formulate a principled, demand-driven methodology for constructing networks from raw data about social organizations. Specifically, given a precise specification of which network-derived quantities the resilience framework will consume (e.g., degree distributions, community structure, temporal motifs, layer correlations, structural balance), determine:

(a) what preprocessing operations on the raw data are necessary and sufficient to yield an interaction, temporal, multilayer, or signed network of the required type;
(b) how the choices made in (a) (thresholds, aggregation windows, sign assignment rules, layer definitions) propagate into the statistical properties of the resulting network and into uncertainty bounds on the resilience estimates;
(c) under what conditions different preprocessing pipelines yield networks that are equivalent for the purposes of the framework, and under what conditions they do not.

Produce both the constructive procedure and the accompanying statistical interpretation.

